## Feature: SPARQL Update removal rules (DELETE)

### Context
The Complex rule builder could only infer triples (SPARQL `CONSTRUCT`). Users also need to
remove triples, for example to hide the meters of a customer that was closed. Removal rules
use SPARQL 1.1 Update forms (`DELETE WHERE`, `DELETE DATA`, `DELETE {…} WHERE {…}`). On
Lakebase and Delta they are stored as overlay rows in the `_overrides` table with origin
`reasoning`, so the source tables are never changed and the matching original triples are
hidden. On Neo4j the matching triples are deleted from the property graph. The SPEC and PLAN
are local and gitignored (`.planning/sparql-update-removal/SPEC.md`, `PLAN.md`).

Decisions applied: origin `reasoning` (D1); a triple a user added by hand is kept (D2, the
plan default); `DELETE DATA` of a missing triple is a `removal_not_found` warning (D3); a
removal must bind a subject or an object (D4); Neo4j removals are not journaled (D5); a
removal that would hide more than 10,000 triples needs an explicit confirmation.

### Changes
1. `src/back/core/reasoning/rules/SparqlPatternCompiler.py` (new): BGP, FILTER and term
   compilation shared by the CONSTRUCT and removal compilers (extracted, no behaviour change).
2. `src/back/core/reasoning/rules/SparqlConstructCompiler.py`: extends `SparqlPatternCompiler`.
3. `src/back/core/reasoning/rules/SparqlUpdateCompiler.py` (new): compiles the three removal
   forms to one `SELECT s, p, o` per template triple. Refuses `INSERT`, `GRAPH`, unbound
   patterns (D4) and any other construct outside the subset.
4. `src/back/core/reasoning/rules/SPARQLRuleEngine.py`: rules carry a `kind` (`construct`
   by default, or `removal`); `validate_rule` dispatches on it; `_execute_one` routes to
   `_execute_construct` or `_execute_removal`; D3 violations; `count_removals` for the
   preview; stats `removed_count`.
5. `src/back/core/reasoning/results/ReasoningResult.py`: `removed_triples` (merged and
   serialised by `to_dict`).
6. `src/back/core/graphdb/overrides.py`: `HIDE_ORIGINS` and `validate_hide_origin`.
7. `src/back/core/graphdb/delta/materialize.py`, `delta/MaterializedStore.py`: origin-aware
   hide (`build_hide_statements`); a reasoning hide skips triples that have a user add row.
8. `src/back/core/graphdb/lakebase/OverrideOpsMixin.py`, `lakebase/FlatStore.py`:
   origin-aware `_hide_triples`; user add rows are dropped only for origin `user`.
9. `src/back/core/graphdb/neo4j/Store.py`, `interfaces/GraphBackend.py`: `delete_triples`
   takes `origin`; a removal is not journaled and skips journaled user adds.
10. `src/back/core/reasoning/ReasoningService.py`: `materialize_inferred` is now
    `_insert_inferred` followed by `_apply_removals` (origin `reasoning`); new
    `count_sparql_removal`.
11. `src/back/objects/digitaltwin/TwinBackgroundTasks.py`,
    `src/back/objects/digitaltwin/build/TwinInferredMaterialize.py`: removed triples are
    forwarded to the graph append path (also when a run has removals only).
12. `src/back/core/reasoning/constants.py`, `src/back/objects/ontology/Rules.py`:
    `REMOVAL_WHERE_RE` and `REMOVAL_TEMPLATE_RE`; reference checks cover removal rules.
13. `src/shared/config/constants.py`: `SPARQL_REMOVAL_CONFIRM_THRESHOLD = 10_000`.
14. `src/api/routers/internal/ontology.py`: `POST /ontology/rules/sparql_rules/removal-preview`
    returns `success`, `count`, `requires_confirmation` and `errors`; nothing is written.
15. Rule editor UI: `src/front/templates/partials/ontology/_ontology_business_rules.html`
    (Rule type selector, removal box with Preview and confirmation);
    `src/front/static/ontology/js/ontology-business-rules.js` (kind handling, preview, save
    gate, removal badge, validation sends the kind); `src/front/static/ontology/css/ontology-business-rules.css`
    (manual layout without the assistant, badge).
16. Docs: `docs/user-guide.md` (Removal Rules section), `docs/api.md` (removal-preview),
    `docs/architecture.md` (`SparqlUpdateCompiler`).

Behaviour to know: a Build clears the rows that reasoning derived (removals included) and
keeps user overrides. Removals come back on the next reasoning run, so a Build alone does not
reapply them.

Known limitations (not decided yet):
- Same-run ordering: removals are applied after the inserts of the same run, so a CONSTRUCT
  rule in that run can still derive from a triple that a removal hides.
- Literals are matched on their lexical form only.
- The preview does not apply D2, so it can over-count triples that have a user add row.
- The Delta materialised table (`materialize_to_delta`) is not changed by removals.

